public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    backtrack(nums, 0, result);
    return result;
}

private void backtrack(int[] nums, int start, List<List<Integer>> result) {
    if (start == nums.length) {
        result.add(Arrays.stream(nums).boxed().collect(Collectors.toList()));
    } else {
        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            swap(nums, start, i);
            backtrack(nums, start + 1, result);
            swap(nums, start, i);
        }
    }
}

private void swap(int[] nums, int i, int j) {
    int temp = nums[i];
    nums[i] = nums[j];
    nums[j] = temp;
}
```
This method uses backtracking to generate all permutations of the input array. The backtracking algorithm is as follows:

1. Start with the first element of the array.
2. Swap the current element with each remaining element.
3. Recursively call the method with the unvisited elements, skipping the current element.
4. Swap the current element with the previously swapped element.
5. If the array is of length 1, add the current element to the output list.

The `swap` method is used to swap the values of two indices in the array.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n!), where n is the length of the input array. This is because there are n! possible permutations of an array of length n, and we need to visit each permutation in order to generate them all. The space complexity is O(n), as we need to store the visited elements in the backtracking recursion.

